Z||000002||Spektyr||04-26-2001||10:47 PM||||I've been over to pontiacs4me's shop and he's done some fiberglass work.  Not sure if he'd consider himself good enough to teach, but I sure as heck couldn't make the things he's done.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||04-27-2001||12:03 AM||||i have build a surfboard and repared many boats.<BR>molds for production[many parts made in them]<BR>must be strong but oneoffs molds can be plaster or thin glass depends on part to be made.<BR>polyester is common resin used, cheapest, stiff but can crack. temp and humidity affect times to set up [kick]as do amt of harder used[use tooo much and it will burn up] hot dry days best time to glass <BR>hot resen will kick in pot mix only a little at a time and use it up. old hot resen will<BR>kick fresh in same pot use waxpaper cups one time only. acidtone to clean up.<BR>glass comes in mat [unwoven],cloth of verring<BR>thickness[weight in oz] heavyest is called roving 24 oz up.<BR> mat and roving together is called fab-mat then you get to exotic carbon fibers or kevar<BR>stronger but $$$$$.<BR>trick is to use just enuff resen to stick mat and cloth but not too much[heavy] or to little [dry spots=weak] ||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||04-27-2001||12:12 AM||||  That's a start, anyone else? I would love to learn more.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
